@@ -0,0 +1,109 @@
+# SymPas summary propagation smoke test
+
+This example checks the current Infer-SymPas prototype on a small C program.
+The return-value slice of `check` should propagate the summary of `helper`, retain
+`a` and the `r < 0` control condition, and not include the unrelated `b` call.
+The `bump`/`check_ptr` pair also exercises basic pointer load/store propagation.
+
+From the repository root:
+
+```bash
+infer --sympas-only --results-dir /tmp/infer-sympas-smoke \
+  -- clang -c examples/sympas/summary_propagation.c
+cat /tmp/infer-sympas-smoke/report.json
+```
+
+Expected high-level findings:
+
+- `helper`: candidate summary `{formal[0]}`;
+- `unrelated`: candidate summary `{formal[0]}`;
+- `check`: candidate summary `{formal[0]}`, with `a` as the propagated dependency
+  and the conditional branch location included.
+- `check_ptr`: candidate summary `{formal[0]}`, propagated through `&x` and `*p`.
+- `choose_first`: candidate summary `{formal[0]}`; the two call sites must not
+  mix `a` and `b` when instantiating `helper`'s summary.
+- `check_global`: retains the dependency on `shared_limit` through `add_limit`.
+
+This is a smoke test, not a completeness evaluation. More precise alias handling,
+recursive calls, richer symbolic summaries, and quantitative slicing evaluation
+remain out of scope for this prototype.
+
+## Multiple call sites
+
+The second example checks a function called with two different actual
+arguments:
+
+```bash
+infer --sympas-only --results-dir /tmp/infer-sympas-multiple-calls \
+  -- clang -c examples/sympas/multiple_calls.c
+```
+
+The prototype should report `helper` with `{formal[0]}` and `choose` with a
+caller-side frontier derived from both call sites. This is a regression smoke
+test, not yet a formal precision proof.
+
+## Global dependency
+
+The third example checks that a return value read from global state is retained
+in the summary:
+
+```bash
+infer --sympas-only --results-dir /tmp/infer-sympas-global \
+  -- clang -c examples/sympas/global_dependency.c
+```
+
+`read_limit` and `check_limit` should report a global dependency in addition to
+the return-value and control-flow facts.
+
+## Loop convergence
+
+The fourth example checks that a loop does not prevent the backward analysis
+from converging and that the returned value still depends on `x`:
+
+```bash
+infer --sympas-only --results-dir /tmp/infer-sympas-loop \
+  -- clang -c examples/sympas/loop.c
+```
+
+## Recursion fixed point
+
+The fifth example checks that recursive calls do not make the interprocedural
+analysis diverge:
+
+```bash
+infer --sympas-only --results-dir /tmp/infer-sympas-recursive \
+  -- clang -c examples/sympas/recursive.c
+```
+
+## Pointer alias
+
+The sixth example checks a local alias chain (`q` aliases `p`) and an indirect
+store/load:
+
+```bash
+infer --sympas-only --results-dir /tmp/infer-sympas-pointer-alias \
+  -- clang -c examples/sympas/pointer_alias.c
+```
+
+## Aggregate memory
+
+The seventh example exercises a struct field, an array initializer, and a
+pointer to a struct. It is a smoke test for aggregate-memory dependencies:
+
+```bash
+infer --sympas-only --results-dir /tmp/infer-sympas-aggregate \
+  -- clang -c examples/sympas/aggregate_memory.c
+```
+
+## Reproducible cost measurement
+
+To record per-case finding counts and elapsed time without claiming a full
+precision benchmark:
+
+```bash
+python3 examples/sympas/evaluate_smoke.py
+```
+
+The script also checks selected expected summaries from
+`expected_dependencies.json`. These are small regression checks, not a
+complete precision/recall benchmark.
